/*​ GENERATE ALL SUBSEQUENCES OF A STRING​Problem StatementGiven a string of length N, generate and print all possible subsequences of the string. A subsequence is a sequence that can be formed by deleting zero or more characters from the original string while maintaining the relative order of the remaining characters. The empty string is also considered a valid subsequence.For example, for the string "abc", the subsequences are: "abc", "ab", "ac", "a", "bc", "b", "c", and "".​Input Format    • The first line contains an integer N, representing the length of the string.    • The second line contains a string S of length N.Output FormatPrint all possible subsequences of the given string, one subsequence per line. The empty subsequence should also be printed.​Constraints    • 1 ≤ N ≤ 20    • S contains only lowercase English letters.    • The string may contain repeated characters.    • Total number of subsequences is 2^N.Sample Input3abc​Sample OutputabcabacabcbcThe last blank line represents the empty subsequence.*/import java.util.Scanner;public class StringSubsequences{	public static void main(String[] args)	{		Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);		String input = sc.next();		generate(input, 0, "");	}	public static void generate(String s, int index, String current)	{	if(index == s.length())	{		System.out.println(current);		return;	}	generate(s, index+1, current+s.charAt(index));	generate(s, index+1, current);	}}/*For the recursive approach to generate all subsequences:​Time ComplexityThere are 2 choices for every character:    • Include the character    • Exclude the characterSo the recursion tree has: 2^N leaf calls.Therefore:  Time Complexity = O(2^N)However, if we consider the cost of creating/printing each subsequence, each subsequence can have up to N characters.So the output-sensitive complexity is: O(N×2^N)For a typical recursion-complexity answer, you can state O(2^N) when focusing on the number of recursive calls.​ ​Space ComplexityThe maximum recursion depth is N:generate(0)  ↓generate(1)  ↓generate(2)  ↓...generate(N)Therefore, recursion stack requires: O(N)If you store all subsequences instead of printing them, the output itself requires: O(N×2^N)​Approach				Time		SpaceGenerate + print			O(N × 2^N)	O(N)Generate + store all subsequences	O(N × 2^N)	O(N × 2^N)For your recursive code that directly prints the subsequences:Time = O(N × 2^N)Space = O(N)*/